-- title:
--   Zero schemes on non-smooth two-line geometric fibres
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a Noetherian commutative ring, $c : C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a proper flat morphism of schemes, $\mathcal V$ a two-affine open cover of $C$ (two affine opens with affine intersection covering $C$), and $U \subseteq C$ an open whose structure morphism $U \hookrightarrow C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$. Let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $c$ (a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R \to C$ with $\varepsilon \circ c = \mathrm{id}$) whose image lies in $U$, let $g, e, r$ be naturals with $g + e = r$, and let $D_\gamma$ be a relative effective Cartier divisor of degree $e$ for $c$ over $\mathrm{id}_{\operatorname{Spec} R}$ — an ideal sheaf datum on $C \times_R \operatorname{Spec} R$ whose associated closed subscheme is finite, flat and locally of finite presentation over the base with fibre rank $e$ at every point — with support contained in the preimage of $U$ under the first projection. Four families of hypotheses on geometric fibres are assumed, indexed by algebraically closed fields $k$ and $k$-points $x$ of $\operatorname{Spec} R$: (i) `hχ`, for every two-affine open cover $\mathcal W$ of the fibre the two-chart Čech Euler characteristic $\dim_k H^0 - \dim_k H^1$ of the pullback to the fibre of $\mathcal{O}(r\varepsilon) \otimes \mathcal{I}_{D_\gamma}$ equals $1$, where $\mathcal{O}(r\varepsilon)$ denotes the dual of the module of the $r$-th power of the kernel ideal of the rigidifying section and $\mathcal{I}_{D_\gamma}$ the ideal module of $D_\gamma$; (ii) `hg`, for every such $\mathcal W$ the space $H^1$ of the structure sheaf of the fibre has dimension $g$; (iii) `hgred`, every geometric fibre $C \times_R k$ is reduced; (iv) `hbad`, a two-line degeneration package on every non-smooth geometric fibre $C \times_R k$: there are two curve models $M_1, M_2$ over $k$ with function field $k(t)$, closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ into the fibre compatible with the structure morphisms, whose images cover the fibre, an $n$, an injective $a : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to k^{\times}$ and a $b : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to k^{\times}$ and a two-affine cover $\mathcal W_0$ of the fibre, such that the point of $M_1$ at the place of $a_i$ and the point of $M_2$ at the place of $b_i$ have the same image, such coincidences are the only ones, $M_1.C \times_{\text{fibre}} M_2.C$ is reduced, the preimages of $\mathcal W_0.U_0$ and $\mathcal W_0.U_1$ in each line are the complements of the place at infinity and of the place at $0$, $i_1$ sends the place at infinity to the point of the fibre cut out by $\varepsilon$, the image of $i_1$ meets the preimage of $U$ exactly in the connected component of that $\varepsilon$-point, the $n$ node images on the first line lie outside the preimage of $U$ while every other point of the fibre lies in it, and the complement of the image of $i_2$ is an open $W_1$ with $i_1$ restricted to its preimage an open immersion; and (v) `hDγcomp`, on every non-smooth geometric fibre the preimage of the image of the subscheme of $D_\gamma$ lies in that same connected component of the $\varepsilon$-point. The conclusion: for every scheme $T$, every locally of finite type $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ and every rigidified line bundle $L$ for $(c,\varepsilon)$ over $t$ (an invertible module on $C \times_R T$ trivialised along the rigidifying section) satisfying `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero`, i.e. on each geometric fibre the pullback of $L$ satisfies `IsAlgEquivZero`, for every algebraically closed $k$ and every $x : \operatorname{Spec} k \to T$ with $C \times_R k$ not smooth over $k$, and every nonzero morphism $\sigma$ from the unit module to the pullback along `mapOnProdOver c x rfl` of $L \otimes (\mathcal{O}(r\varepsilon) \otimes \mathcal{I}_{D_\gamma})$ (with $D_\gamma$ first pulled back along $t$), there is a relative effective Cartier divisor $D_x$ of degree $g$ for $c$ over $x \circ t$ whose ideal sheaf datum is the zero-scheme ideal of $\sigma$ — the infimum of those ideal sheaf data dominating the ideal generated by the coefficients of $\sigma$ on every affine open — and which is supported in $U$.
--
--   This is the non-smooth (degenerate) half of the statement that a nonzero section of the twisted chart module on a geometric fibre cuts out a relative effective Cartier divisor of degree $g$ supported in the smooth locus $U$; the smooth fibres are treated separately, and the two cases are combined in [`AlgebraicGeometry.RelPicard.exists_relEffCartierDiv_I_eq_zeroSchemeIdeal_chartModule_fibre`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.RelPicard.exists_relEffCartierDiv_I_eq_zeroSchemeIdeal_chartModule_fibre). The degeneration hypothesis `hbad` encodes the semistable picture of two projective lines glued transversally at finitely many nodes, with $\varepsilon$ on the first line, which is the geometric input used to compute the zero scheme there.
-- source:
